
# 1. Introduction

Deploying an application successfully is only one part of running applications on OpenShift.

Real-world environments frequently encounter issues during:

- Build
- Deployment
- Networking
- Storage
- Configuration
- Security
- Runtime

The objective of troubleshooting is to quickly identify the root cause and restore application availability.

---

# 2. General Troubleshooting Approach

Always troubleshoot layer by layer.

```
Application

↓

Container

↓

Pod

↓

Deployment

↓

Service

↓

Route

↓

OpenShift

↓

Infrastructure
```

Never jump directly to conclusions.

---

# 3. Useful OpenShift Commands

View Pods

```bash
oc get pods
```

View detailed Pod information

```bash
oc describe pod <pod-name>
```

View logs

```bash
oc logs <pod-name>
```

Follow logs

```bash
oc logs -f <pod-name>
```

View previous container logs

```bash
oc logs --previous <pod-name>
```

Execute inside Pod

```bash
oc exec -it <pod-name> -- sh
```

View Events

```bash
oc get events
```

Describe Deployment

```bash
oc describe deployment <deployment-name>
```

---

# 4. Pod Status

Common Pod states

### Pending

Pod has not yet been scheduled.

Possible reasons

- Insufficient resources
- PVC unavailable
- Node unavailable

---

### ContainerCreating

Container image is being prepared.

Possible reasons

- Image download
- Volume attachment
- Secret mounting

---

### Running

Application is running normally.

---

### CrashLoopBackOff

Container starts

↓

Application crashes

↓

Container restarts

↓

Crash repeats

Possible reasons

- Application exception
- Invalid configuration
- Missing environment variables
- Database unavailable

---

### ImagePullBackOff

OpenShift cannot pull the container image.

Possible reasons

- Incorrect image name
- Missing registry credentials
- Image does not exist
- Registry unavailable

---

### ErrImagePull

Initial image pull failure.

Usually followed by

ImagePullBackOff.

